 \subsection{Positioning of This Work}
 \label{subsec:rel_position}
 
-The literature establishes that UPF realizations expose markedly different energy--performance profiles, that threshold logic becomes harder to engineer as the configuration space grows, and that digital twins enable safe RL training. What remains underexplored is measurement-grounded, learning-based UPF orchestration that jointly addresses heterogeneous realization modes, explicit cooldown guards, asymmetric reconfiguration costs, and limited horizontal scaling within a single control formulation. This paper fills that gap using a physically profiled digital twin, a constraint-aware PPO controller, and a comparative evaluation against static and rule-based baselines.
+The existing literature shows that different UPF realizations exhibit distinct energy--performance trade-offs, that threshold-based control becomes increasingly difficult to design as the configuration space grows, and that digital twins provide a practical way to train RL policies safely. However, measurement-grounded approaches to UPF orchestration that combine these aspects in a single framework remain limited.
+
+In particular, the joint treatment of heterogeneous realization modes, explicit cooldown constraints, asymmetric reconfiguration costs, and horizontal scaling within a unified control formulation has received relatively little attention. This work addresses this gap by combining a physically profiled digital twin with a constraint-aware PPO controller and a systematic comparison against static and rule-based baselines.
 
